			<item>
				<title>Arrow vs Parquet: When to Use Which</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/engineering/arrow-vs-parquet/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 15 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/engineering/arrow-vs-parquet/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-short-answer&#34;&gt;The short answer&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Parquet&lt;/strong&gt; is a storage format — it&amp;rsquo;s designed to sit on disk (or S3) efficiently.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;&lt;strong&gt;Arrow&lt;/strong&gt; is an in-memory format — it&amp;rsquo;s designed to move data between systems without copying.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You&amp;rsquo;ll often use both: read a Parquet file into an Arrow table, process it in memory, write it back as Parquet.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;parquet&#34;&gt;Parquet&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Parquet is a columnar file format. Because it stores data column-by-column rather than row-by-row, it compresses extremely well and lets query engines skip entire columns they don&amp;rsquo;t need.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Python Dataclasses: A Practical Guide</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/engineering/python-dataclasses/</link>
				<pubDate>Fri, 01 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/engineering/python-dataclasses/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;why-dataclasses&#34;&gt;Why dataclasses&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Before &lt;code&gt;@dataclass&lt;/code&gt; (added in Python 3.7), a simple data-holding class meant writing &lt;code&gt;__init__&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;__repr__&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;__eq__&lt;/code&gt; by hand. Dataclasses generate all of that for you.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-python&#34; data-lang=&#34;python&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; dataclasses &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; dataclass&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;@dataclass&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Pipeline&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    name: str&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    schedule: str&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    enabled: bool &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;That&amp;rsquo;s it. You get a constructor, a readable repr, and equality comparison for free.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;defaults-and-default-factories&#34;&gt;Defaults and default factories&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-python&#34; data-lang=&#34;python&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; dataclasses &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; dataclass, field&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;@dataclass&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Pipeline&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    name: str&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    tags: list[str] &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; field(default_factory&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;list)  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# mutable default — must use field()&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;default_factory&lt;/code&gt; pattern catches people out: you can&amp;rsquo;t write &lt;code&gt;tags: list = []&lt;/code&gt; because the same list object would be shared across all instances.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
